1
00:00:00,120 --> 00:00:06,380
Imagine trying to renovate, um, a sprawling mansion without any blueprints.

2
00:00:06,420 --> 00:00:10,650
You know, you are basically taking a sledgehammer to a wall just to see if it is load-bearing.

3
00:00:10,800 --> 00:00:14,440
Right. Which means any mistakes are gonna be incredibly costly to change.

4
00:00:14,520 --> 00:00:21,040
Exactly. And honestly, that is exactly how governments have been treating their multi-billion dollar IT systems.

5
00:00:21,050 --> 00:00:28,360
I mean, you walk into a ministry to modernize their tech, and without a map, those systems inevitably become fragmented, siloed, and duplicated.

6
00:00:28,440 --> 00:00:36,360
Yeah, we see that all the time, and that is exactly why, uh, for today's deep dive, we are looking at the PAERA framework.

7
00:00:36,480 --> 00:00:39,180
Right, the Public Administration Ecosystem Reference Architecture.

8
00:00:39,380 --> 00:00:46,760
Yes. And specifically, we really need to focus on section four point six, which is their published taxonomy of public bodies.

9
00:00:46,860 --> 00:00:51,350
Because the golden rule of this architecture, it completely shifts your whole approach.

10
00:00:51,720 --> 00:00:56,230
I mean, it states that you must classify any public body before you even attempt to model it.

11
00:00:56,460 --> 00:01:02,580
Because, you know, knowing that classification instantly reveals the body's capabilities, its data domains, and its governance.

12
00:01:02,589 --> 00:01:06,560
So you basically have a blueprint in hand before a single interview even takes place.

13
00:01:06,600 --> 00:01:12,680
Exactly, yes. Okay, so if classifying is the shortcut to getting that blueprint,

14
00:01:13,660 --> 00:01:17,720
uh, we need to know what categories we are actually sorting these bodies into.

15
00:01:18,420 --> 00:01:23,010
Well, the framework divides the vast majority of government into three main types.

16
00:01:23,230 --> 00:01:25,160
So the first one is the policy unit.

17
00:01:25,340 --> 00:01:26,680
Right, the policy unit.

18
00:01:26,740 --> 00:01:30,180
And this typically sits right at the core of a ministry.

19
00:01:30,220 --> 00:01:36,520
I mean, they own the rules, and they set the policy, but crucially, they do not run services at scale.

20
00:01:37,040 --> 00:01:42,760
Oh, I see. So they are the architects drawing up the plans, not the actual builders pouring the concrete.

21
00:01:42,830 --> 00:01:46,140
That is a great way to put it. They just decide what those services should look like.

22
00:01:46,240 --> 00:01:51,510
Okay, but if they aren't dealing with the public directly, I mean, someone has to be enforcing the rules they write, right?

23
00:01:51,560 --> 00:01:54,800
Yeah, yeah. And that falls to the second type, which is the regulatory agency.

24
00:01:54,860 --> 00:01:57,720
Right, okay. They license, they supervise, and they enforce.

25
00:01:58,120 --> 00:02:07,460
So when you interact with the regulatory agency, you know immediately they will have an appeals process, and they absolutely must hold a register of the entities they regulate.

26
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:07,840
Got it.

27
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:13,280
Which I guess leaves the actual day-to-day heavy lifting with the public to the third type.

28
00:02:13,460 --> 00:02:18,180
Yes. The service delivery authority, I mean, they are the ones running the services at scale.

29
00:02:18,220 --> 00:02:22,160
So you are looking at, what, high transaction volumes, case files, queues.

30
00:02:22,260 --> 00:02:26,600
Exactly. And you know, all those frontline systems that interact directly with citizens.

31
00:02:26,780 --> 00:02:28,380
Wait, let me push back on this a little bit.

32
00:02:28,800 --> 00:02:32,150
If you are walking into a ministry to map their systems,

33
00:02:32,780 --> 00:02:36,440
you can't just assume every regulatory agency acts the exact same way.

34
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:41,420
Doesn't assuming this expected profile lead to blind spots?

35
00:02:41,500 --> 00:02:43,980
Well, it is not about assuming they are all identical.

36
00:02:44,020 --> 00:02:46,300
It is really about establishing a baseline.

37
00:02:46,360 --> 00:02:47,260
Okay, how so?

38
00:02:47,340 --> 00:02:49,540
Each type comes with an expected profile.

39
00:02:49,580 --> 00:02:56,660
So if you classify a body as a regulatory agency, you walk in the door expecting a licensing capability and an enforcement record.

40
00:02:56,800 --> 00:03:00,740
Oh, so you spend your interview time confirming or correcting that specific profile.

41
00:03:00,880 --> 00:03:03,740
Mm-hmm. Rather than, you know, trying to build one from absolute zero.

42
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:06,560
Exactly. It completely reframes the whole discovery phase.

43
00:03:06,880 --> 00:03:12,590
Yeah, you aren't just sitting there asking, "Well, what do you do?" You're asking, "How do you perform this specific regulatory function?"

44
00:03:12,700 --> 00:03:16,660
Precisely. It saves so much time and prevents the system from getting fragmented.

45
00:03:16,780 --> 00:03:20,480
But looking at this blueprint, I feel like there is a missing piece here.

46
00:03:21,060 --> 00:03:24,920
Where do the really massive central systems fit in?

47
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:32,640
Like a national identity system or a land register doesn't neatly fit into a single policy unit or a frontline service.

48
00:03:32,900 --> 00:03:35,880
Right. Well, those are what we call the supporting elements.

49
00:03:35,920 --> 00:03:39,150
Okay. If the three main body types are the rooms in your mansion,

50
00:03:39,950 --> 00:03:44,380
these elements are the foundation and the plumbing that support the entire structure.

51
00:03:44,480 --> 00:03:46,940
Oh, that makes sense. So how do those break down?

52
00:03:46,980 --> 00:03:50,880
They break down into two categories. First, you have state registers.

53
00:03:50,960 --> 00:03:53,720
Their entire existence boils down to just one job.

54
00:03:53,960 --> 00:03:54,460
Which is what?

55
00:03:54,760 --> 00:03:58,480
Being the authoritative single source of truth for one specific thing.

56
00:03:58,680 --> 00:04:02,560
So like a population register, a business register, or a land register.

57
00:04:02,960 --> 00:04:05,340
It is the one single place the truth actually lives.

58
00:04:05,560 --> 00:04:09,600
You got it. And the second category consists of shared platforms.

59
00:04:09,700 --> 00:04:11,160
Like what, what would be an example of that?

60
00:04:11,400 --> 00:04:18,810
Think of identity verification or payment gateways or data exchange systems that are used across many different government bodies.

61
00:04:19,019 --> 00:04:22,550
Right. And I feel like a critical distinction to make here is about ownership,

62
00:04:23,160 --> 00:04:25,409
because if these are the foundation and the plumbing,

63
00:04:26,480 --> 00:04:31,160
you know, a single ministry really cannot just hoard the national identity platform.

64
00:04:31,360 --> 00:04:34,990
No, absolutely not. They must be treated as shared building blocks.

65
00:04:35,160 --> 00:04:43,160
Right. If a ministry treats a state register or a shared platform as its own private property, the data inevitably gets duplicated.

66
00:04:43,240 --> 00:04:44,500
Oh, because they are hoarding it.

67
00:04:44,600 --> 00:04:51,820
Exactly. It gets captured twice, rekeyed manually in different departments, and the government just ends up locked into one supplier.

68
00:04:52,080 --> 00:04:56,780
Wow. So this means misclassification isn't just like a typo on an org chart.

69
00:04:56,840 --> 00:04:59,980
Yeah. It is actually a massive architectural red flag.

70
00:05:00,040 --> 00:05:09,940
Yes. If you spot a body that is supposed to be a neutral register, but it is acting like a policy unit, you know, shaping rules to suit itself or hoarding data, the taxonomy

71
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:12,300
exposes that misalignment immediately.

72
00:05:12,380 --> 00:05:13,220
That is fascinating.

73
00:05:13,230 --> 00:05:18,620
And that is exactly why adopting a published framework like PAERA is so critical.

74
00:05:18,680 --> 00:05:25,960
Right, because it ensures that when someone says service delivery authority, every single architect in the country pictures the exact same thing.

75
00:05:26,060 --> 00:05:29,320
Exactly. You aren't inventing a scheme from scratch.

76
00:05:29,920 --> 00:05:35,960
You adopt the standard which prevents those fragmented siloed systems from ever being built in the first place.

77
00:05:36,040 --> 00:05:38,480
It creates a shared objective language. I love that.

78
00:05:39,080 --> 00:05:45,220
But you know, if the architecture of a government's data systems naturally exposes when a neutral register oversteps its boundaries,

